                <h2>Why Daily Compliance Audits Matter</h2>
<p>In outbound advertising, account bans, creative rejections, and delivery restrictions are among the most frequent operational incidents. These issues are rarely caused by a single factor—instead, they're usually the result of accumulated risks: neglected account settings, creatives that violate policies, or targeting strategies that trigger platform risk controls.</p>
<p>Building a daily audit habit transforms firefighting into proactive defense. This SOP breaks compliance checks into four modules. Spending just 15 minutes daily on these checklists can significantly reduce violation triggers.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Module 1: Account Status Audit</h2>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Objective: Ensure the account is healthy with no suspicious logins or unauthorized permission changes.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Verify login IP and device match your usual operating environment (flag any异地登录 alerts immediately)</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm account balance is sufficient and top-ups have arrived on time</li>
<li>[ ] Validate payment methods are active and credit cards aren't expiring soon</li>
<li>[ ] Review any new user permissions added to Business Manager—confirm they went through approval</li>
<li>[ ] Check Account Quality Rating for any decline</li>
<li>[ ] Scan for official policy violation notifications from the platform</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Response Protocol:</strong></p>
<p>If you detect unfamiliar login locations, change passwords immediately and enable two-factor authentication. If account quality drops for two consecutive days, pause high-spend campaigns for internal review.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Module 2: Creative Pre-Screening</h2>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Objective: Block high-risk content before formal submission.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Text layer:</strong> Avoid absolute language ("cheapest," "only," "100% effective")</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Image layer:</strong> Confirm portrait usage has proper authorization; be extra cautious with celebrity/influencer imagery</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Landing page:</strong> Above-the-fold content must match ad promises—no bait-and-switch to unrelated pages</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Audio/video:</strong> Verify background music has commercial licensing; narration tone shouldn't be exaggerated or fear-based</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>CTA buttons:</strong> No misleading phrasing ("You've won!" "Claim your prize now")</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Tool Assistance:</strong></p>
<p>Before submission, use the platform's preview tool to simulate multi-placement display. Confirm the text and creative combination renders correctly across all ad placements. One utility app was rejected because its CTA read "Click to claim reward" but actually required a minimum purchase—resulting in creative rejection and an account warning.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Module 3: Campaign Policy Red Lines</h2>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Objective: Verify active campaigns aren't using prohibited targeting methods or delivery patterns.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Audience exclusion logic is correctly configured—no gaps in targeting filters</li>
<li>[ ] Delivery regions are on the whitelist; certain categories have special restrictions in specific countries</li>
<li>[ ] No sudden spikes in exposure (e.g., 300%+ spend increase within one hour)</li>
<li>[ ] Landing page loads normally; mobile experience is smooth</li>
<li>[ ] No use of platform-flagged "restricted" ad types (certain engagement-baiting formats)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>High-Risk Scenario Alerts:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Promotional ads around holidays need extra scrutiny on "price comparison" and "discount percentage" labeling rules</li>
<li>Health and financial ads require landing pages with corresponding certification documents</li>
<li>Ads targeting children must strictly segment age groups, avoiding audiences under 13</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Module 4: Anomaly Alerts and Rapid Response</h2>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Objective: Build a closed-loop mechanism for detection, response, and post-incident review.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Alert Triggers (respond if any condition is met):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Official violation notification received</li>
<li>Creative rejections exceed 3 times/day</li>
<li>Single-account spend drops 50%+ without clear external factors</li>
<li>User complaints received about misleading ad content</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Response SOP:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Immediately pause</strong> relevant Campaign or Ad Set</li>
<li>Screenshot and save violation notices and rejection reasons</li>
<li>Cross-reference policy documentation to identify specific violation points</li>
<li>Revise and resubmit—avoid submitting identical problematic creatives</li>
<li>Complete internal post-incident review within 24 hours and update the compliance checklist</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Closing</h2>
<p>Compliance isn't a one-time task—it's ongoing risk management. We recommend embedding this checklist into your team's daily operations, using fixed routines to reduce random risks. Review and update the checklist quarterly, adding new items as platform policies evolve.</p>
<p>If your team currently lacks a systematic audit mechanism, start with Module 1 "Account Status Audit." Most teams see noticeable improvements in account stability within two weeks.</p>
